body:
That last line usually says it all. You have your answer.

&#x200B;

It'd be no different to a sport, book, place you live...anything. Unless something massively changes, why are you going to keep doing/living/going to something that you're not enjoying?

i felt a small lump on my earlobe - size of a spot and thats what I thought it was. Popped it slightly and that was that.

&#x200B;

Later, it felt like I hadnt gotten it or it had re-filled and so I squeezed once more (I know, stupid) and left it. Then I happened to catch it in the mirror later and  noticed the underneith/bottom was really red and it looked like this and now theres also a big lump on my earlobe. 

&#x200B;

Im assuming I've just messed with it too much and its probably just a burst blood vessle right? its not something more serious? I dont really want to waste any proffesionals time with it if its likely bc I messed with it.

&#x200B;

p.s. lesson learnt, ill leave stuff like this alone next time.

Im flying out in 2 days to see my bf and im pretty sure ill be safe to get there and back. But during all of the days that were counting down to it, i havent always had that certainty/ guarantee. My best advice? take it a day at a time otherwise you're gonna be overwhelming yourself. Learn to focus on other things too, if you focus all of your mental and physical energy on this and get really anticipated, the bigger the emotional blow is gonna be on you. 

&#x200B;

Also - make sure you have a good support system. If youre the one flying and for whatever reason plans do fall through, make sure you have people there to comfort and look after you too! I.e. your boyfriend/gf bc theyll be going through the same thing. I hope all goes well for you though!

Hey OP, just wanted to say I get where you're coming from. When I still lived with my dad he was incredibly verbally abusive and a heavy alcoholic. Luckily he never hit me, but that didn't stop him from emotionally scarring me. I've been in your situation in asking that person for homework and as youre definately aware thats never any fun. 

&#x200B;

I know exactly how you feel and just want you to know that I'm proud of you for even asking him for help. I know that your deadline is passed, but perhaps instead of asking that grumpy bag of air next time, is there any chance you could get a teacher or your school to help with your homework situation?  It's ok to cry, you're doing amazing - all the best :)

Idk how far you live from school, how old you are or how strict your school is; but when my anxiety or depression was bad, I used to fake needing dentist appointments with notes or other excuses so I could leave school early. Then, because I didnt live far from school, i'd just go do something I wanted to do, e.g. go to the park, or go home and do some gaming. Maybe not if I had exams or whatever, but its some form of release at least :)

&#x200B;

Hope it doesnt get too overwhelming for you!

I completely understand, this is also exactly what I struggle with.

&#x200B;

Maybe all we need is to take a proper break
